---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageMeta from '@/components/PageMeta.astro';
import AuthorityPanel from '@/components/ia/AuthorityPanel.astro';
import JourneyNav from '@/components/ia/JourneyNav.astro';
---

<Layout title="Evidence, provenance and qualified mappings" description="How SPDTF 2.0 turns attributed evidence into context-owned candidates and distinguishes five mapping meanings.">
  <PageMeta category="Ontology journey" variant="brand" updated="SPDTF 2.0 development · evidence and traceability" />
  <h1>Evidence, provenance and qualified mappings</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Evidence becomes a context-owned candidate through attribution, competency questions,
    review and a recorded disposition. The word “mapping” is always qualified because
    different records connect different things for different purposes.
  </p>
  <AuthorityPanel destination="spdtf-2" />
  <JourneyNav />

  <h2 id="loop">From evidence to a governed output</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Attribute the participant, external, PDTF 1.0 or machine source and its handling status.</li>
    <li>Frame a question, scope and context owner; keep uncertainty and counterexamples visible.</li>
    <li>Draft glossary, dictionary, taxonomies, controlled vocabularies, resources and relationships.</li>
    <li>Record coverage, provenance, constraints, candidate changes and open questions.</li>
    <li>Invite review, record the disposition and preserve an immutable candidate cut.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    AI may extract, compare and draft. It cannot select the authority, approve a meaning
    or promote a candidate. Source data, derived evidence, machine proposals and human
    approvals remain distinguishable in the receipt.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mapping">Five qualified mapping meanings</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Do not collapse these records under an unqualified “mapping” label</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Qualified meaning</th><th scope="col">What it connects</th><th scope="col">Standing</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">PDTF 1.0 RML schema–ontology verification</th><td>Published schema structures and the PDTF 1.0-derived ontology.</td><td>Retained implementation and traceability evidence.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Planned legacy JSON-LD contexts</th><td>Legacy JSON property names and ontology terms for compatibility views.</td><td>Planned or retained migration work; not new semantic authority.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SPDTF 2.0 cross-context semantic mappings</th><td>Related but non-identical meanings at bounded-context boundaries.</td><td>Candidate agreements owned by Interoperability and reviewed by the affected groups.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Property Pack source-item coverage links</th><td>Required source items and candidate constructs.</td><td>Coverage evidence only; <strong>not RML or R2RML</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Optional deployment transformation runtime</th><td>Operational transformations between representations where a deployment needs one.</td><td>Optional implementation service; it cannot decide semantic meaning.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="receipts">Evidence receipt</h2>
  <p>
    A candidate receipt should let an auditor identify the source, context owner, question,
    proposed change, affected output, review record, disposition, standards-profile
    version and validation scope. A link from PDTF 1.0 to SPDTF 2.0 must say whether it
    is evidence, corroboration, compatibility, comparison, projection or supersession.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/spdtf-2/ontologies/validation">Next: validation, conformance and generated projections →</a></p>
</Layout>
